The only literature import is existence of a planar constant-width completion:
every finite planar diameter-`D` set is contained in a convex body of constant
width `D`. The exact statement was checked in Kupitz–Martini–Wegner,
*A Linear-Time Construction of Reuleaux Polygons*, Beitr. Algebra Geom. 37
(1996), 415–427, **p. 421**, in the proof of Theorem 0.1, citing their
reference [5], Theorem 54, p. 126. [Original article](https://emis.de/ft/11518).
We use existence only; the article's separate uniqueness theorem assumes a
Hamiltonian diameter graph and is not used.

The support-energy bump in PR #615,
`target_c_global_diameter_star_cubic_budget_2026-10-02.md`, was inspected.
Its additive actual-star budget is acknowledged as prior unpromoted work.
Here the entire needed energy calculation is rederived. No theorem of that PR
is a premise. The new application is to **short attachment clusters with small
curvature mass**, not forced zero-curvature intervals at actual diameter stars.

Section 75 already proves `c_I=O(D^(2/3))`. The older unreviewed
`target_c_nearcircle_no_bounded_harmonic_components_2026-09-09.md` proves a
qualitative minimum-component divergence under a near-circle hypothesis.
Neither is claimed as new. The new statements are the explicit individual
cube-root bound, the `D`-dependent counting window, and the final
`O(sqrt(D))` count after using the attachment-edge capacity.

All source points, including contact isolates, are retained. `I_r` denotes
only an unloaded component of the positive nonisolated contact core.

## 2. Local normals of actual diameter endpoints

Let the complete source lie in a radius-`R<D` disk centered at `o` and put
`r_0=D-R`. If `b` is an actual diameter endpoint with partner `b'`, then

\[
n_b=(b-b')/D
\]

is a strict outward supporting unit normal of the complete source hull:

\[
n_b\cdot(b-x)\ge {|b-x|^2\over2D}>0\quad(x\ne b).
\tag{2.1}
\]

Also `|b-o|>=r_0`, and

\[
(b-o)\cdot n_b
={|b-o|^2+D^2-|b'-o|^2\over2D}\ge r_0.
\tag{2.2}
\]

Thus its angle from the radial direction at `b` is at most
`alpha=arccos(r_0/R)`. Two endpoint radial directions whose source points
are at distance at most `L<2r_0` differ by at most
`gamma=2 arcsin(L/(2r_0))`. Consequently chosen actual diameter normals of
a cluster of diameter `L` have pairwise angular separation at most

\[
2\alpha+\gamma.
\tag{2.3}
\]

For a global minimizer with `D>=4096`, take the safe radius bound
`R<25D/48`, since `3^(1/4)<4/3` and `sqrt(D)>=64`.
Then `r_0>23D/48`. Whenever `L<=3`, or whenever
`D>=65536` and `L<=sqrt(D)/100`, the quantity
`t=L/(2r_0)` is less than `1/1000`. Hence

\[
\cos(\alpha+\gamma/2)
>{23\over25}{999\over1000}-{10\over25}{1\over1000}
={22967\over25000}>{\sqrt3\over2}.
\tag{2.4}
\]

The square of the final inequality is certified by rational arithmetic.
It follows that (2.3) is strictly less than `pi/3`. Such a finite normal
family lies in an ordinary arc of length `<pi/3`: lift relative to one
normal to `(-pi/3,pi/3)`; the extremes differ by less than `2pi/3<pi`,
so their ordinary difference is itself their minor separation.

### Lemma 2.1 — at most two loaded unit neighbors

In every unit-separated global minimizer with `D>=4096`, every unit circle
contains at most two actual diameter endpoints.

**Proof.** Three such points form a triangle of circumradius one. Each
side is at least one, so each interior angle is at least `pi/6` and
therefore at most `2pi/3`. Every exterior normal-cone angle is at least
`pi/3`. Strict supporting normals chosen one at each of the three vertices
cannot all lie in an arc of length `<pi/3`: in their cyclic normal order,
the arc from the first to the last passes through the full normal cone of
the middle vertex. But their source cluster has diameter at most two,
and (2.3)–(2.4) put the chosen diameter normals in just such an arc.
Contradiction. This proof does not require triangle-freeness. ∎

### Lemma 2.2 — a loaded contact leaf has an unloaded neighbor

For a global minimizer with `D>=4096`, if a positive-diameter vertex `b`
has exactly one positive-contact neighbor `x`, then `x` is not an actual
diameter endpoint.

**Proof.** Suppose it is. Its chosen diameter normal `n_x` has angle
`<pi/3` from **every** positive diameter normal at `b`, by (2.3) for the
unit pair. Therefore

\[
n_x\cdot g_b>0,
\qquad g_b=\sum_{by}\beta_{by}(b-y).
\]

Equilibrium gives `g_b=alpha_bx(b-x)`, with `alpha_bx>0`.
On the other hand (2.1) at `x` gives
`n_x dot (b-x)<=-1/(2D)<0`. Contradiction. ∎

## 3. Completion energy with a small curvature mass

Choose any constant-width-`D` completion `W` containing the whole source.
Let `h` be its support function, `p=h-D/2`, and
`rho=h+h''`. In curvature-measure notation,

\[
p(\theta+\pi)=-p(\theta),\qquad
\rho(\theta)+\rho(\theta+\pi)=D.
\tag{3.1}
\]

Nonnegative curvature measures and (3.1) give a density
`0<=rho<=D`; in particular `h` is in `W^(2,infinity)`.
The support-area formula gives

\[
\delta_W={\pi D^2\over4}-\operatorname{area}(W)
={1\over2}E(p),\qquad
E(g)=\int_0^{2\pi}[(g')^2-g^2].
\tag{3.2}
\]

On antiperiodic `H^1` functions, `E` is positive semidefinite: only odd
Fourier frequencies occur, with coefficients proportional to `j^2-1`.
Its kernel consists of the first harmonics (translations). With
`f=p+p''=rho-D/2`, polarization and integration by parts give

\[
\langle f,g\rangle^2\le2\delta_W E(g).
\tag{3.3}
\]

For an interval `J=(-a,a)`, define

\[
g_J(t)=\cos t/\cos a-1\quad(t\in J),
\]

its negative antipodal copy on `J+pi`, and zero elsewhere. It is a
continuous antiperiodic `H^1` function. Put `k=tan a-a`. Direct integration
on each interval, including the zero endpoint values, gives

\[
E(g_J)=4k,\qquad
\langle f,g_J\rangle
=2\int_J\rho g_J-2Dk.
\tag{3.4}
\]

Suppose `L>=2`, `a=1/(8L)`, `D>=10000 L^2`, and
`int_J rho <=2L`. Since `a<=1/16`,

\[
0\le g_J\le\sec a-1\le a^2,
\quad k\ge a^3/3,
\quad 4La^2\le Dk.
\tag{3.5}
\]

The secant estimate follows from `cos a>=1-a^2/2`; the tangent estimate
follows by integrating `tan^2 t>=t^2`. Thus (3.4) is at most `-Dk`.

If `N` intervals of the same length `2a` have their intervals and
antipodal copies pairwise disjoint in interior, then their bumps have
disjoint supports. Applying (3.3) to their sum yields

\[
\boxed{
\delta_K\ge\delta_W\ge {ND^2k\over8}
\ge {ND^2\over12288 L^3}.}
\tag{3.6}
\]

This is a simultaneous inequality. No individual deficit inequalities are
summed without first establishing disjoint support.

## 4. Each actual harmonic attachment cluster supplies such an interval

Fix `I_r`, its distinct loaded attachment set `A_r`, and an upper bound
`diam(A_r)<=L>=2`. Assume `D>=65536` and `L<=sqrt(D)/100`.
Choose one actual diameter normal at each attachment. By Section 2 their
normal arc has span `delta<pi/3`.

Let `w` be its bisector, with tangent `Jw`. Supporting inequalities at
two attachments and the decompositions
`n_i=cos(theta_i)w+sin(theta_i)Jw`, `|theta_i|<=delta/2`, imply

\[
\operatorname{width}_{\operatorname{conv}A_r}(w)
\le L\tan(\delta/2).
\tag{4.1}
\]

Indeed for their difference `d`, one supporting inequality bounds `w dot d`
below by `-tan(theta_i)Jw dot d`, and the other bounds it above by
`-tan(theta_j)Jw dot d`; `|Jw dot d|<=L`.

Harmonic trapping puts `I_r` and all its contact neighbors in
`conv(A_r)`. An unloaded vertex has at least three unit neighbors, so
canonical Lemma 75.1 gives width at least `1/2` in **every** direction.
Consequently

\[
\delta\ge2\arctan(1/(2L))\ge1/(2L).
\tag{4.2}
\]

The completion is supported at the same actual attachment by each of the
chosen diameter normals: (2.1) holds for all `x in W` because `W` has
diameter `D` and contains the attachment's diameter partner.
Take the extreme normals and their support points. Along the intervening
completion boundary arc, tangent projection onto the bisector tangent is
at least `cos(delta/2)>1/2`. The chord of its two support points is at
most `L`; hence

\[
\int_{\text{whole normal arc}}\rho\le2L.
\tag{4.3}
\]

Choose any subinterval of length `1/(4L)=2a` inside this arc.
Equations (4.2)–(4.3) establish all hypotheses of (3.6) for `N=1`.

### Theorem 4.1 — uniform individual cube-root diameter

For every canonical triangle-free C-II source with `D>=65536`, every
unloaded positive-contact component satisfies

\[
\boxed{(\delta_r+2)^3>{D\over3072\pi\sqrt3}.}
\tag{4.4}
\]

**Proof.** Set `L=delta_r+2`. If `L<=sqrt(D)/100`, combine (3.6) and
`delta_K<(pi sqrt(3)/4)D`. If `L>sqrt(D)/100`, the same conclusion is
automatic: `3072 pi sqrt(3)>25^3`, and `D^(1/6)>4` in this range, so
`sqrt(D)/100>(D/(3072 pi sqrt(3)))^(1/3)`. ∎

The identical proof works for `L=max(2,diam(A_r))`; it also gives a
cube-root lower bound on the **physical** attachment diameter, once the
right-hand side exceeds eight. This is stronger than divergence of graph
diameter alone. No smooth disk limit is assumed.

## 5. Simultaneous counting windows and the square-root count

Use the eventual canonical fat force hull in Lemma 75.3. For an integer
`M>=0`, put `L=M+2`, assume `L<=sqrt(D)/100`, and let `N_M` count
components with `delta_r<=M`. Section 75.2 puts the attachments of each
counted component in a circular boundary-arclength window of radius
`w=22L` around any representative attachment. Its hypotheses hold here:
`D>=65536` and `L<=sqrt(D)/100` imply `L<=D/10000`.

Choose a maximal collection of representatives separated by more than
`2w`, retaining one component at each chosen representative. Let its
size be `K`. Their radius-`w` windows are disjoint. Every other
representative is within `2w` of a chosen one. Such a covering window
has arclength `4w`, contains at most `4w+1` unit-separated loaded
vertices, and each vertex represents at most three components. Therefore

\[
N_M\le3(88L+1)K\le267LK.
\tag{5.1}
\]

The chosen components supply normal intervals of length `1/(4L)` by
Section 4. These intervals have disjoint interiors: chosen attachment
sets lie in disjoint source-hull boundary windows, and their strict
supporting normals occur in the same cyclic normal order. This argument
uses actual attachment hull vertices; it does not identify a scaffold
with the source.

An interval of this fixed length can overlap the antipodal copies of at
most two other intervals of the same length with disjoint interiors.
Thus the antipodal-overlap graph has maximum degree two. Greedy selection
retains at least `K/3` intervals whose supports together with antipodal
copies have pairwise disjoint interiors. Apply (3.6) to this selection,
and use the canonical area allowance, to obtain

\[
K<9216\pi\sqrt3\,{L^3\over D}.
\]

Together with (5.1) this proves the finite window inequality

\[
\boxed{N_M<C_W{(M+2)^4\over D},\qquad
C_W=2460672\pi\sqrt3.}
\tag{5.2}
\]

The constants are deliberately coarse. When its right side is below one,
(5.2) means `N_M=0`; if there are no selected representatives, the strict
bound is immediate.

### Intermediate bound — exponent 3/5 without attachment-edge charging

Every sufficiently large canonical triangle-free C-II source satisfies

\[
\boxed{c_I=O(D^{3/5}).}
\tag{5.3}
\]

More precisely, whenever the finite-window hypotheses above hold,

\[
\boxed{c_I<C_W{(M+2)^4\over D}
+{2+2\pi D\over M+1}.}
\tag{5.4}
\]

**Proof.** Components not counted by `N_M` have integer graph diameter
at least `M+1`; the canonical sum-diameter ledger bounds their number
by `(2+2pi D)/(M+1)`. This proves (5.4). Taking
`M=floor(D^(2/5))` gives (5.3). For all sufficiently large `D`, this
choice satisfies `M+2<=sqrt(D)/100`. The fat-hull threshold is the
canonical nonexplicit one; no numerical Level I threshold is inferred. ∎

### Exact scalar consequences

The stronger result below changes the error orders in Section 75's identities:

\[
e_{BB}+s_C=q+k+O(\sqrt D),\qquad
m_B+k=c_B+s_C+O(\sqrt D),
\]

\[
\mu_DD=q+s_C-2k+O(\sqrt D)
=2q-e_{BB}-k+O(\sqrt D).
\tag{5.5}
\]

These remain necessary ledgers; no sign contradiction follows from them.

### Theorem 5.1 — finite dyadic attachment capacity

For an integer `M>=1`, count components in the diameter band
`M<=delta_r<2M`, and put `L=2M+2`. Under the same fat-hull hypotheses,
`D>=65536`, and `L<=sqrt(D)/100`, their number `B_M` satisfies

\[
\boxed{B_M<C_B{(2M+2)^3\over D},\qquad
C_B=7372800\pi\sqrt3.}
\tag{5.6}
\]

**Proof.** Repeat the maximal separated representative selection, with
radius `w=22L`. Each component assigned to a chosen representative has
its own representative within `2w` and all its attachments within `w`
of that representative. Thus every attachment of every assigned
component lies in the radius-`3w` boundary window about the chosen one.
It contains at most `6w+1=132L+1` loaded vertices.

The canonical **edge** identity is

\[
t_r=\delta_r+3+s_r+r_r\ge M+3.
\tag{5.7}
\]

Each attachment edge ends at a loaded vertex. The full loaded contact
degree is at most three, so all assigned components together use at
most `3(132L+1)` such edges. Therefore the number assigned to one
chosen representative is at most

\[
{3(132L+1)\over M+3}<800.
\tag{5.8}
\]

Here `L=2M+2`; the ratio is bounded independently of `M`. Repeated
attachments to one loaded vertex are counted as separate edges and
are paid by its full degree, so no identification of incidence
occurrences is used. Chosen representative windows are disjoint;
the normal-interval and antipodal selection of Section 5 still gives
`K<9216 pi sqrt(3)L^3/D`. Multiply by 800 to prove (5.6). ∎

### Theorem 5.2 — all-phase square-root component count

Every sufficiently large canonical triangle-free C-II source satisfies

\[
\boxed{c_I=O(\sqrt D).}
\tag{5.9}
\]

An explicit eventual bound is

\[
\boxed{c_I<
\left(800\pi+{512C_B\over7\cdot400^3}\right)\sqrt D
+{800\over\sqrt D}.}
\tag{5.10}
\]

**Proof.** There are no singleton unloaded components for `D>=4096`,
by Lemma 2.1 and the unloaded degree lower bound. Take `D>=262144`
large enough for the canonical fat-hull conclusion. Let `2^J` be the
largest power of two at most `sqrt(D)/400`. For every band
`M=1,2,...,2^J`, with only powers of two included,

\[
L=2M+2\le\sqrt D/100.
\]

Thus (5.6) applies. Since `2M+2<=4M`, the sum of these band counts is
at most

\[
{64C_B\over D}\sum_{j=0}^J2^{3j}
<{512C_B\over7D}(2^J)^3
\le {512C_B\over7\cdot400^3}\sqrt D.
\tag{5.11}
\]

Every remaining component has graph diameter at least `2^(J+1)`,
which is greater than `sqrt(D)/400`. The canonical sum-diameter
budget therefore bounds their number by

\[
{400(2+2\pi D)\over\sqrt D}
=800\pi\sqrt D+800/\sqrt D.
\]

This proves (5.10) and (5.9). The constants are conservative; the
nonexplicit canonical fat-hull threshold remains. ∎

The upgrade from (5.4) to (5.9) is not an inference from cell
disjointness alone. It uses the exact load `t_r>=delta_r+3`, grouping
by comparable graph diameters, and the simultaneous loaded degree
capacity. It does not require a new theorem about attachment-cell
area or a positive KKT multiplier floor.

## 6. The tree contact-core branch is impossible above an explicit diameter

### Theorem 6.1

A canonical triangle-free C-II source with `D>=4096` has a cycle in its
nonisolated contact core.

**Proof.** Suppose the whole nonisolated contact core is a forest with
`c>=1` components. Then `e=v-c`. Canonical ZF gives
`e+h>=2v-2`, while Woodall gives `h<=q`; hence
`v+c-2<=q`. For `u=v-q`, the number of unloaded core vertices, this
is `c+u<=2`. The only possibilities are `(c,u)=(1,0),(1,1),(2,0)`.

If `u=0`, every vertex is loaded, so a leaf in any tree component has
a loaded contact neighbor, contradicting Lemma 2.2. If `(c,u)=(1,1)`,
call the unloaded vertex `p`. Every leaf is loaded and must be adjacent
to `p`, again by Lemma 2.2. A finite tree all of whose leaves are
adjacent to one vertex is a star: any larger component after deleting
that vertex has a leaf not adjacent to it. The unloaded center has
contact degree at least three, while Lemma 2.1 permits at most two
loaded unit neighbors. Contradiction. Thus the contact core is not a
forest and contains a cycle. ∎

Lemma 2.1 also implies that every unloaded vertex has an unloaded contact
neighbor. Thus the graph induced by the unloaded core has no singleton
components for `D>=4096`. Theorem 4.1 gives the substantially stronger
quantitative conclusion for larger `D`.

## 7. Hostile checks and failed closing attacks

- A connected gap graph does not supply a private leaf release. Positive
  KKT circulation restores an opposing nonbasis diameter across the same
  cut. This existing regression remains binding.
- Weighted contact energy by itself absorbs the packet reactions. The
  exact virial identity `sum alpha=D^2` supplied no new loss, so no
  stress-selection lower bound or finite release is claimed here.
- The suggested convex pure-rhombic perfect-matching shortcut was already
  present in `target_c_post_zf_perfect_matching_exclusion_2026-09-09.md`.
  It is not a new result of this run.
- Unit square: its positive contact core is cyclic; no unloaded component
  is present. It is retained.
- Regular pentagon plus its center: an exact triangle-free six-point
  source has a positive tree contact core, full mixed rigidity, and an
  affine PSD stress kernel. Its diameter is
  `sqrt((5+sqrt(5))/2)<2`, so it demonstrates why Theorem 6.1 needs a
  large-diameter hypothesis. It is not asserted globally optimal here.
- All claims about `c_I` concern positive-contact components only.
  Contact isolates neither acquire a harmonic equation nor disappear.
- Original normal intervals are not enough for addition: antipodal
  overlap is explicitly removed with the degree-two selection.
- Arbitrarily many interior points or component populations are not
  controlled by (5.9). Large components remain.

For completeness, the pentagon-star regression consists of the center
and five equally spaced points on the unit circle. Its only contacts
are the five spokes; the outer adjacent squared distance is
`s^2=(5-sqrt(5))/2>1`, and its five outer skip-one pairs have squared
diameter `d^2=(5+sqrt(5))/2<4`. Give each spoke weight `alpha=d^2/5`
and each diameter weight `beta=1/5`. The centered Gram matrix in the
verifier certifies vertex equilibrium. Eliminating the center from the
stress gives the outer Schur matrix

\[
{d^2\over5}(I-\mathbf1\mathbf1^T/5)-L_H(1/5).
\]

Its constant and coordinate modes vanish; its two remaining Fourier
modes have eigenvalue `(d^2-s^2)/5=sqrt(5)/5>0`. The center pivot is
`d^2>0`, so the full stress is PSD with affine kernel of dimension
three. For mixed rigidity, fix the center velocity. Each spoke forces
the outer velocity to be `t_i Jp_i`. A skip-one diameter row forces
`t_i=t_(i+2)`, since `sin(4pi/5)!=0`. The diameter five-cycle makes
all rates equal, leaving only rotation; restoring translation gives
mixed rank `2*6-3=9`.

The first attempted closing bridge after Theorem 5.2 was a bound on
the number of components of a **selected diameter forest** by `c_I`.
It fails even with positive equilibrium, an affine PSD kernel, and full
mixed rigidity. A regular unit pentagon has no unloaded vertices, so
`c_I=0`; two disjoint actual diameters form a forest with two components.
The same mechanism occurs on every regular odd unit shell. These are
linear-population shells, without the actual complete O–O grid or a
large global-optimality certificate. They reject a bridge from stress
and component counts alone, not the actual train-port coupling target.
The exact pentagon instance is checked by the verifier.

## 8. Computation and proof status

[Exact verifier](verify_target_c_harmonic_cubic_window_2026_10_02.py):
`python3 research/verify_target_c_harmonic_cubic_window_2026_10_02.py`.
It checks the rational threshold/coefficient inequalities, the normal
and secant margins, the intermediate and final exponent bookkeeping,
dyadic attachment capacities, fixed-length antipodal
selection on exact circle intervals, and the square/pentagon-star stress
regressions with arithmetic in `Q(sqrt(5))`. It does not certify general
completion geometry, global optimality, the canonical stack, or Level I;
those geometric implications have the analytic proofs above. The
verifier is corroboration, not an unlisted theorem premise.

## 9. Frontier effect and next attack

The proposed frontier change is an all-phase **individual** cube-root
attachment lower bound and an improvement of the component-count error
from `O(D^(2/3))` to `O(sqrt(D))`, plus explicit tree-core exclusion.
No canonical state change is made before independent audit.

The first next attack was a constant multiplicity version of (5.1).
The dyadic edge-capacity argument above resolves it **for comparable
diameters**, which is enough for the square-root count. A constant
multiplicity assertion for all diameters at once is not used.

The precise next closing obligation is to couple the actual threshold
diameter-forest components and their extreme-layer carriers to these
same harmonic attachment components, with a charge to
`e_BB+2s_C-3k` or a source-preserving finite motion. Their square-root
counts alone do not identify the two decompositions.

The square-root component count does not establish a finite competitor. The
open-cell exact-phase, threshold staircase, nonconstant-phase swaps,
split-star O–O, inactive-interface, higher-surplus full-core, and
macroscopic inactive-container/exterior-cap branches remain uncovered.

Promotion recommendation: **unreviewed** pending independent hostile
audit. A promotion would require the full state-version, route, prompt,
dependency, certificate, and generated-view transaction; this report
does not perform it.
